---
import { SITE_URL, latestDay, dateFormatted, RATES } from '../lib/site';
import { buildFooter, buildHeader, CHROME_STYLE } from '../lib/chrome';

const pageTitle = 'Data Methodology | U.S. Treasury Yield Rates';
const pageDescription = 'How YieldWatch sources, validates, and publishes U.S. Treasury yield curve data, including update timing, maturity coverage, and known limitations.';
const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}/methodology/`;
const latest = latestDay();
const dataDate = latest ? dateFormatted(latest.date) : null;
const historyStart = RATES.length ? dateFormatted(RATES[0].date) : '1990';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'WebPage',
      '@id': `${pageUrl}#page`,
      url: pageUrl,
      name: pageTitle,
      description: pageDescription,
      isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#site` },
      about: { '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#org` },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Dashboard', item: `${SITE_URL}/` },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Methodology', item: pageUrl },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

const html = `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>${pageTitle}</title>
  <meta name="description" content="${pageDescription}">
  <link rel="canonical" href="${pageUrl}">
  <meta name="robots" content="index, follow">
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <meta property="og:title" content="${pageTitle}">
  <meta property="og:description" content="${pageDescription}">
  <meta property="og:url" content="${pageUrl}">
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="YieldWatch">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
  <meta name="twitter:title" content="${pageTitle}">
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="${pageDescription}">
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 100 100'%3E%3Crect fill='%236366f1' rx='15' width='100' height='100'/%3E%3Cpath d='M20 70 L35 55 L50 65 L65 40 L80 45' stroke='white' stroke-width='8' fill='none' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'/%3E%3Ccircle cx='80' cy='45' r='6' fill='white'/%3E%3C/svg%3E">
  <link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="Daily Treasury Yield Summaries RSS" href="/feed.xml">
  <script type="application/ld+json">${JSON.stringify(schema)}</script>
  ${CHROME_STYLE}
  <style>
    :root { --bg-primary: #0a0a0f; --bg-secondary: #0f0f18; --bg-card: #16161f; --border-color: #2a2a3a; --text-primary: #f0f0f5; --text-secondary: #a0a0b0; --text-muted: #606070; --accent: #6366f1; --accent-hover: #818cf8; }
    * { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body { margin: 0; background: var(--bg-primary); color: var(--text-primary); font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif; line-height: 1.7; }
    main { max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem 3rem; }
    h1 { font-size: 2.1rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
    h2 { font-size: 1.3rem; margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem; color: var(--accent-hover); }
    h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 0.4rem; }
    p, li { color: var(--text-secondary); }
    a { color: var(--accent-hover); }
    .lede { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.08rem; }
    .card { background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 10px; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
    .card p { margin: 0.35rem 0; }
    ul, ol { padding-left: 1.25rem; }
    li { margin: 0.3rem 0; }
    table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 1rem 0; font-size: 0.95rem; }
    th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color); color: var(--text-secondary); }
    th { color: var(--text-primary); }
    code { background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 4px; padding: 0.1rem 0.35rem; font-size: 0.9em; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  ${buildHeader(null)}
  <main>
    <h1>Data Methodology</h1>
    <p class="lede">This page documents exactly where YieldWatch data comes from, when it updates, how it is validated, and what it does not cover. The goal is that any number on the site can be traced back to a public official source.</p>

    <div class="card">
      <p><strong>Primary source:</strong> U.S. Department of the Treasury, Daily Treasury Par Yield Curve Rates (home.treasury.gov).</p>
      <p><strong>Latest published data:</strong> ${dataDate ?? 'see dashboard'}</p>
      <p><strong>Historical coverage:</strong> Daily observations beginning ${historyStart}.</p>
      <p><strong>Update cadence:</strong> Business days only, after the Treasury publishes its rates, typically around 3:30 PM Eastern Time.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>YieldWatch reads the Treasury's published daily par yield curve feed and stores each business day's official rates. No third-party market data, broker quotes, or intraday estimates are used for the official daily series. The same feed supplies the dashboard, the daily summaries, and the JSON API.</p>
    <p>The Treasury publishes par yield curve rates, which represent the average yield on outstanding Treasury securities at each maturity. These are not constant-maturity bond-equivalent yields and are not the same as intraday market quotes.</p>

    <h2>2. Maturity coverage</h2>
    <p>The site publishes the maturities the Treasury reports, including bills, notes, and bonds:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Bucket</th><th>Maturities</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Bills</td><td>4-week, 6-week, 1-month, 2-month, 3-month, 4-month, 6-month</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Notes</td><td>1-year, 2-year, 3-year, 5-year, 7-year, 10-year</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bonds</td><td>20-year, 30-year</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Not every maturity is published on every day. If the Treasury omits a tenor, the site shows the tenors that were published rather than filling gaps with estimates.</p>

    <h2>3. Update process</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>The daily job retrieves the Treasury's published feed for the latest business day.</li>
      <li>Rates are parsed and checked against the expected maturity set and plausible ranges.</li>
      <li>The stored series, static API snapshots, charts, and per-post metadata are regenerated.</li>
      <li>The daily summary is written from the verified numbers and published with the rest of the site.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If the Treasury feed is unavailable or a value fails validation, the previous day's data remains in place and the build is skipped rather than publishing a partial curve.</p>

    <h2>4. Daily summaries</h2>
    <p>Each daily summary is generated from the same verified rates used on the dashboard. The summary is limited to describing observed changes: level shifts, spreads between maturities, and whether the curve steepened, flattened, or inverted. It does not forecast future rates and does not contain trading recommendations.</p>
    <p>Numbers quoted in a summary are checked against the stored rate record before publication. Headlines are derived from the largest day-over-day move so that each post has a distinct, factual title.</p>

    <h2>5. Validation and quality checks</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Maturity labels must match the Treasury's published set.</li>
      <li>Values must parse as numbers and fall within plausible historical bounds.</li>
      <li>Date continuity is checked so a new row cannot silently replace an existing day.</li>
      <li>Published summaries are checked for numeric agreement with the underlying record.</li>
      <li>Builds fail closed: a failed validation stops publication instead of shipping bad data.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Known limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Business days only.</strong> Weekends and federal holidays have no new official rate.</li>
      <li><strong>Daily close, not live.</strong> The series reflects the Treasury's published daily figures, not real-time trading.</li>
      <li><strong>Revisions.</strong> The Treasury can revise or correct published values. When that happens, the stored record is updated and the affected summary may be regenerated.</li>
      <li><strong>Par yields.</strong> These are par yield curve rates, not zero-coupon or constant-maturity series.</li>
      <li><strong>No intraday tracking.</strong> Any future intraday indicator will be labeled separately and never presented as an official daily rate.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. API and reuse</h2>
    <p>The same data is available through the free <a href="/api-docs/">JSON API</a>. Responses are static snapshots regenerated with each daily update, so values match the dashboard. Reuse is permitted with attribution to YieldWatch and, where practical, to the U.S. Department of the Treasury as the original source.</p>

    <h2>8. Corrections</h2>
    <p>If you find a discrepancy, email <a href="mailto:hello@yieldwatch.io">hello@yieldwatch.io</a> with the date and maturity. Corrections are checked against the Treasury's published record and applied at the source, so the dashboard, summaries, and API stay consistent.</p>

    <p class="meta">See also: <a href="/about/">About YieldWatch</a> and the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a>.</p>
  </main>
  ${buildFooter()}
</body>
</html>`;
---

<Fragment set:html={html} />
